      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the equation of the line parameterized by \\( t \\) such that the vector on the line at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and the vector on the line at \\( t = 3 \\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\). After finding the equation of the line, we can use it to find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\).\n\n### Step 1: Find the direction vector\nThe direction vector \\(\\mathbf{D}\\) of the line can be found by taking the difference between the vectors at \\( t = 3 \\) and \\( t = 2 \\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{D} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Write the parametric equation of the line\nThe parametric equation of the line can be written as:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2t \\\\ 4 - 8t \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nHere, \\( x = 1 + 2t \\) and \\( y = 4 - 8t \\).\n\n### Step 3: Find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\)\nSubstitute \\( t = -7 \\) into the parametric equation to get the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2(-7) \\\\ 4 - 8(-7) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 14 \\\\ 4 + 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThus, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
