      "text": "To find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\), we first need to determine the parametric equation of the line. The parametric equation of a line can be written in the form:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\mathbf{r}_0 + t \\mathbf{d}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{r}_0\\) is a point on the line and \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is the direction vector of the line. We know two points on the line: when \\( t = 2 \\), the vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), and when \\( t = 3 \\), the vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\). First, we find the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\).\n\nThe direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) can be found by subtracting the vector at \\( t = 2 \\) from the vector at \\( t = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow we can write the parametric equation of the line as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (t - 2) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTo find the vector when \\( t = -7 \\), we substitute \\( t = -7 \\) into the parametric equation:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(-7) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (-7 - 2) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (-9) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ 72 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 18 \\\\ 4 + 72 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
